    "q-65330": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65330",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{2}$ is not in the domain of the function.\n\nII. $y=10$ is in the range of the function. $$\n\nIII. $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is a vertical asymptote.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263803/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is not defined for $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=10$ is in the range.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, thus $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is not a vertical asymptote.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
