    "q-158053": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158053",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ then $6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x+8\\pi )+7=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263803/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x+8\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x+8\\pi )+7 & =6(-\\frac{5}{3})+7 \\\\  & =-10+7 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
